WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.470
The experiments are organized as an ablation across
five scenarios that isolate each design decision.

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:10.751
Scenario zero is the raw-data baseline with no
privacy protection.

00:00:10.831 --> 00:00:15.745
Scenario one uses a single shared autoencoder to
encode one combined dataset.

00:00:15.825 --> 00:00:19.896
Scenario two simulates two peers, each with its own
autoencoder,

00:00:19.976 --> 00:00:22.036
whose embeddings are then joined.

00:00:22.116 --> 00:00:27.873
Scenarios three and four repeat one and two but
make the autoencoder non-naive: it becomes

00:00:27.953 --> 00:00:32.931
a multitask network that also predicts the
objective variable during encoding.

00:00:33.011 --> 00:00:38.055
The clear pattern is that the non-naive variants
beat their naive counterparts.

00:00:38.135 --> 00:00:39.612
On Buzz in Social Media,

00:00:39.692 --> 00:00:45.254
the test R-squared rises from about ninety-one and
a half percent with the naive shared

00:00:45.334 --> 00:00:49.664
autoencoder to about ninety-four percent with the
non-naive version,

00:00:49.744 --> 00:00:54.139
and on MNIST accuracy climbs from eighty-eight to
ninety-one percent.

00:00:54.219 --> 00:00:58.290
Guiding the latent space with the target variable
clearly helps.
